1. Dynamics AX has evolved over versions - AX 2009, AX 2012, and Dynamics 365 Finance & Operations (D365 F&O)
2. REST services are more commonly associated with D365 F&O rather than older AX versions
3. In AX 2012, you typically create AIF (Application Integration Framework) services
4. In D365 F&O, you create OData entities or custom services

Let me provide a comprehensive answer that covers the most relevant scenarios:

1. For D365 F&O - Custom REST API using service contracts
2. For AX 2012 - AIF Services (which can expose SOAP/WCF services)

I'll focus on D365 F&O since that's where REST services are more commonly implemented, but I'll also mention AX 2012 approach.

Let me create a practical example with proper X++ code structure.
[/Thinking]

# Creating a Custom REST Service in Dynamics 365 Finance & Operations (X++)

Dynamics 365 Finance & Operations (D365 F&O) supports custom REST services using service contracts and data contracts. Below is a complete guide to building a custom REST API in X++, including best practices and deployment steps.

---

## 1. Create a Service Contract Class

```xpp
[SysEntryPointAttribute(true)]
class CustRESTService
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Get customer information by account number
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="_custAccount">Customer account number</param>
    /// <returns>Customer data contract</returns>
    [SysHttpServiceAttribute(HttpMethod::Get, "customers/{custAccount}")]
    public CustContract getCustomer(str _custAccount)
    {
        CustTable custTable;
        CustContract contract;

        select firstOnly custTable
            where custTable.AccountNum == _custAccount;

        if (custTable)
        {
            contract = new CustContract();
            contract.parmAccountNum(custTable.AccountNum);
            contract.parmName(custTable.Name);
            contract.parmCurrency(custTable.Currency);
        }

        return contract;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Create a new customer
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="_contract">Customer data contract</param>
    /// <returns>Created customer contract</returns>
    [SysHttpServiceAttribute(HttpMethod::Post, "customers")]
    public CustContract createCustomer(CustContract _contract)
    {
        CustTable custTable;
        CustContract returnContract;

        ttsbegin;

        custTable.clear();
        custTable.initValue();
        custTable.AccountNum = _contract.parmAccountNum();
        custTable.Name = _contract.parmName();
        custTable.Currency = _contract.parmCurrency();
        custTable.insert();

        ttscommit;

        returnContract = new CustContract();
        returnContract.parmAccountNum(custTable.AccountNum);
        returnContract.parmName(custTable.Name);
        returnContract.parmCurrency(custTable.Currency);

        return returnContract;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Update customer information
    /// </summary>
    [SysHttpServiceAttribute(HttpMethod::Put, "customers/{custAccount}")]
    public CustContract updateCustomer(str _custAccount, CustContract _contract)
    {
        CustTable custTable;
        CustContract returnContract;

        select forUpdate custTable
            where custTable.AccountNum == _custAccount;

        if (custTable)
        {
            ttsbegin;
            custTable.Name = _contract.parmName();
            custTable.Currency = _contract.parmCurrency();
            custTable.update();
            ttscommit;

            returnContract = new CustContract();
            returnContract.parmAccountNum(custTable.AccountNum);
            returnContract.parmName(custTable.Name);
            returnContract.parmCurrency(custTable.Currency);
        }

        return returnContract;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Delete customer by account number
    /// </summary>
    [SysHttpServiceAttribute(HttpMethod::Delete, "customers/{custAccount}")]
    public void deleteCustomer(str _custAccount)
    {
        CustTable custTable;

        select forUpdate custTable
            where custTable.AccountNum == _custAccount;

        if (custTable)
        {
            ttsbegin;
            custTable.delete();
            ttscommit;
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 2. Create a Data Contract Class

```xpp
[DataContractAttribute]
class CustContract
{
    str accountNum;
    str name;
    str currency;

    [DataMemberAttribute]
    public str parmAccountNum(str _value = accountNum)
    {
        accountNum = _value;
        return accountNum;
    }

    [DataMemberAttribute]
    public str parmName(str _value = name)
    {
        name = _value;
        return name;
    }

    [DataMemberAttribute]
    public str parmCurrency(str _value = currency)
    {
        currency = _value;
        return currency;
    }
}
```

---

## 3. Optional: Add Error Handling

```xpp
[SysHttpServiceAttribute(HttpMethod::Get, "customers")]
public List getCustomers()
{
    CustTable custTable;
    List customerList = new List(Types::Class);

    try
    {
        while select custTable
        {
            CustContract contract = new CustContract();
            contract.parmAccountNum(custTable.AccountNum);
            contract.parmName(custTable.Name);
            contract.parmCurrency(custTable.Currency);
            customerList.addEnd(contract);
        }
    }
    catch (Exception::Error)
    {
        throw error("Failed to retrieve customers");
    }

    return customerList;
}
```

---

## 4. Deploy and Test the Service

### Deployment Steps:
1. Build the project in Visual Studio.
2. Deploy the project to the D365 F&O environment.
3. Run database synchronization if needed.

### Testing with Postman:

**Get Customer:**
```
GET https://[your-instance].cloudax.dynamics.com/data/CustRESTService/customers/US-001
Authorization: Bearer [token]
```

**Create Customer:**
```
POST https://[your-instance].cloudax.dynamics.com/data/CustRESTService/customers
Authorization: Bearer [token]
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "accountNum": "US-002",
    "name": "New Customer",
    "currency": "USD"
}
```

---

## 5. For Dynamics AX 2012 (AIF Services)

In AX 2012, custom services are exposed via AIF (Application Integration Framework), typically using SOAP/WCF.

```xpp
[SysEntryPointAttribute(true)]
class CustAIFService
{
    public CustContract getCustomer(str _custAccount)
    {
        CustTable custTable;
        CustContract contract;

        select firstOnly custTable
            where custTable.AccountNum == _custAccount;

        if (custTable)
        {
            contract = new CustContract();
            contract.parmAccountNum(custTable.AccountNum);
            contract.parmName(custTable.Name);
        }

        return contract;
    }
}
```

**To expose in AX 2012:**
1. Right-click the class → Properties → Set `ServiceGroup` and `ServiceName`.
2. Deploy the service via AIF.
3. Configure endpoints in the AIF portal.

---

## 6. Best Practices

- Use `[SysEntryPointAttribute(true)]` for security and transaction control.
- Validate all input parameters.
- Use `ttsbegin`/`ttscommit` for data integrity.
- Implement error handling with try/catch blocks.
- Follow naming conventions for contracts and service classes.
- Use OData entities for standard CRUD when possible.
- Secure services using OAuth 2.0 in D365 F&O.

---

## 7. Authentication

D365 F&O uses OAuth 2.0. Obtain a token from Azure AD and include it in the `Authorization` header:

```
Authorization: Bearer <access_token>
```

---

This guide provides a complete and production-ready approach to building custom REST services in Dynamics 365 Finance & Operations using X++. Adjust the logic and contracts based on your business requirements.

